# Templates are meant to be committed.
!.env.example

# ── Secrets — never commit ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Committed signing material is a liability: it survives a visibility change, a
# fork, or a collaborator's clone, and git history keeps it after deletion. The
# RELEASE keystore is the app's identity — with it and its passwords anyone can
# publish an update appearing to come from Coopvest.
#
# The previous file said `# Allow keystore files to be committed (user choice)`
# with the patterns commented out, which is how the release keystore ended up in
# a PUBLIC repository. The rules are active now.
android/keystore/
android/key.properties
*.jks
*.keystore

# The Android DEBUG keystore is deliberately NOT tracked but also deliberately
# NOT a secret: the SDK generates it with the same well-known alias and password
# for every developer, so it grants no authority. It is excluded because it is a
# build artifact that regenerates on demand, not because it is sensitive.
android/app/debug.keystore

# Live credential files.
